A HardwareBuffer texture (patch 0006) tells its producer when the GPU has read
what each frame drew of it (board the-galaxy-tab-canvas-went-slow-on-build-1095,
위험 1). The producer showed a buffer once its own GPU had finished it, and
wrote it again as soon as it was not shown — while the raster thread, a layer
tree behind (pipeline depth 2), and the GPU after it could still be reading it
for the frame that showed it.

createHardwareBufferTexture(buffer, readSignal) takes a native function the
reads are told to: `void (*)(int64_t texture_id, int32_t kind, uint64_t count)`.
The texture counts the paints that drew its image. Once a frame is submitted
(Rasterizer, after the frame's Submit — Texture::OnFrameSubmitted, every
texture alike), the Impeller Vulkan texture tells "submitted through count"
(kind 0) and submits an empty command buffer whose completion — its fence
covers what the queue took before it — tells "read through count" (kind 1),
on error too, so no producer stands waiting on a failed submit. A frame that
did not draw the texture tells nothing: the last frame that drew it told its
own reads. The GL texture tells nothing, and its producer sees no reads to
wait for. A readSignal of 0 asks for none.

The producer's side (gpu_engine's ScreenTexture) writes a buffer again only
once two UI frames have begun since the last layer tree that showed it — the
pipeline has submitted that tree then — and the reads have caught up with
what was submitted.

